# coding: latin-1
"""
Cross-platform collateralized-loan risk assessment (crypto collateral).

Not a traditional credit score: a risk-of-undercollateralization check for
a loan backed by crypto collateral. The only quantity genuinely private to
more than one party is the client's existing debt on OTHER lending
platforms -- everything else (prices, volatility, on-chain wallet
behaviour, market liquidity) is public data, computed by a Python layer
outside MP-SPDZ (see Scripts/risk_data/) and fed in as private input of the
REQUESTING platform only, because MP-SPDZ has no live network access during
a secure computation and Programs/Public-Input/ (the framework's public-
input channel) does not work in the binary circuit domain (PUBINPUT is
absent from GC/instructions.h) -- see Compiler.compilerLib for the binary
dummy-type list that would otherwise silently misbehave.

All arithmetic is sint (integers), no sfix: every ratio test is a
cross-multiplication against a public policy constant instead of a secret
division, which sidesteps the whole class of sfix/binary-domain portability
bugs found while building the previous program in this repo.

Three reveal modes (compile-time arg <mode>, see decide_* below):
  0 = benchmark (default): decide_public() reveals every sub-score, both
      policy thresholds, and all 7 intermediate boolean flags to all N
      parties. Portable to all three domains -- this is the only mode
      compiled for the binary domain, and the only one exercised by
      Scripts/mpc_bench.py's automated sweeps.
  1 = strict: decide_strict() composes the whole decision in SECRET
      arithmetic and reveals only a 0/1/2 code, and only to REQUESTER via
      reveal_to(). A thesis case study on the cost of maximal privacy
      (see the field/ring-only limitation below), not meant for
      production use -- REQUESTER alone learns the outcome, so no
      multi-party sign-off on a shared result is possible.
  2 = production: decide_production() also composes in secret arithmetic
      (so the 7 flags never exist in the clear to any party, not even
      transiently), but reveals the decision code, the total score,
      max_ltv_bps, AND liq_threshold_bps to ALL N parties via plain
      .reveal() -- enough for every participating platform to
      independently verify and sign the same outcome, and to derive an
      accurate (not just conservative) indicative liquidation price (see
      Scripts/crypto_risk_run.py's on-chain-publish step and
      Scripts/risk_data/policy.py) -- without exposing the 5 sub-scores
      or any of the 7 flags that would reveal the internal weighting of
      the risk model. liq_threshold_bps was withheld in an earlier
      revision on the assumption that withholding more was automatically
      safer; it derives only from max_ltv_bps (itself already revealed)
      plus PUBLIC policy constants, never from the cross-platform debt
      this program actually protects, so withholding it bought no
      privacy -- only a needlessly noisy liquidation-price estimate. Same
      N==2-vs-N>=3 exposure rule as mode 0, with no exception: at N==2
      only the leverage band is ever revealed, to anyone, including
      REQUESTER -- giving REQUESTER the exact aggregate would let it
      recover the other platform's exact debt by subtraction (it already
      knows its own contribution and the new loan), independent of who
      the reveal target is.

Modes 1 and 2 are field/ring only: both depend on composing the decision
in secret arithmetic BEFORE any reveal, which is only proven safe outside
the binary domain (decide_public's docstring explains why composing
secret booleans arithmetically before revealing silently broke in an
earlier program in the binary domain specifically). Mode 1 additionally
needs reveal_to(), unavailable for binary-circuit types
(Compiler/GC/types.py). Requesting mode 1/2 while compiling for binary
raises CompilerError at compile time rather than failing in a more
confusing way later.

Input file order (Player-Data/Input-P<i>-0), whitespace separated:
  i != REQUESTER:
    existing_debt_usd
  i == REQUESTER, in this exact order:
    existing_debt_usd        (its own slot in the platform loop above)
    new_loan_usd              private, the loan being underwritten
    collateral_value_usd       collateral_amount x spot price
    vol_30d_bps                annualized volatility, basis points
    corr_bps                   collateral vs benchmark asset, 90d, bps
    liquidity_ratio_bps        24h volume / collateral_value, bps
    wallet_age_days
    wallet_flags                count of on-chain red flags (0 = clean)

Usage: ./compile.py [domain flags] crypto_collateral_risk <n_platforms=2> <requester=0> <mode=0>
"""

# Deliberately NOT importing sint/print_ln/etc: see credit_score.mpc's note
# in prior revisions -- the compiler injects domain-configured versions of
# these names into this file's exec namespace, an explicit import would
# grab the wrong (non-domain-configured) version. CompilerError is a plain
# exception class, not one of those domain-configured names, so importing
# it explicitly is safe.
from Compiler.exceptions import CompilerError

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compile-time parameters
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

N_PLATFORMS = 2
if len(program.args) > 1:
    N_PLATFORMS = int(program.args[1])

REQUESTER = 0
if len(program.args) > 2:
    REQUESTER = int(program.args[2])

MODE_BENCHMARK = 0   # decide_public: full detail, all N parties, all domains
MODE_STRICT = 1      # decide_strict: code only, REQUESTER only, field/ring
MODE_PRODUCTION = 2  # decide_production: code+score+max_ltv, all N parties, field/ring

MODE = MODE_BENCHMARK
if len(program.args) > 3:
    MODE = int(program.args[3])

if MODE != MODE_BENCHMARK and program.options.binary:
    raise CompilerError(
        f'mode={MODE} (strict/production) requires the field or ring '
        f'domain, not binary: both modes depend on composing the '
        f'decision in secret arithmetic before any reveal, which is not '
        f'proven safe in the binary domain (see decide_public\'s '
        f'docstring), and mode=1 additionally needs reveal_to(), which '
        f'Compiler/GC/types.py raises CompilerError for unconditionally '
        f'on binary-circuit types. Compile with mode=0 for binary, or '
        f'drop -B/-G for mode=1/2.')

if not program.options.binary:
    program.use_edabit(True)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Policy constants: PUBLIC, compile-time, versioned. Never read as input
# from any party -- these are the lender's underwriting rules, not data
# belonging to a client or a single platform. All N parties must agree on
# them *before* participating, which in practice means verifying the
# compiled bytecode/source hash against a published policy version, not
# trusting a value any single party could otherwise inject at runtime.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

POLICY_VERSION = 1  # bump whenever any constant below changes

BPS = 10000

# Volatility tiers -> (max_ltv_bps, volatility sub-score out of 20)
VOL_STABLE_BPS = 500      # < 0.5% daily-return stdev, annualized -- stablecoin-like
VOL_BLUECHIP_BPS = 3000   # < 30% -- BTC/ETH-like
VOL_MEDIUM_BPS = 6000     # < 60% -- mid-cap
MAX_LTV_STABLE_BPS = 8500
MAX_LTV_BLUECHIP_BPS = 6500
MAX_LTV_MEDIUM_BPS = 5000
MAX_LTV_HIGH_BPS = 3500
VOL_SCORE_STABLE = 20
VOL_SCORE_BLUECHIP = 18
VOL_SCORE_MEDIUM = 10
VOL_SCORE_HIGH = 3

LIQ_BUFFER_BPS = 1000        # liquidation threshold = max_ltv + this buffer

CORR_LOW_BPS = 3000
CORR_MED_BPS = 6000
CORR_SCORE_LOW = 15
CORR_SCORE_MED = 8
CORR_SCORE_HIGH = 0

LIQUIDITY_GOOD_BPS = 100000   # 24h volume >= 10x collateral value
LIQUIDITY_OK_BPS = 20000      # >= 2x
LIQUIDITY_MIN_BPS = 5000      # >= 0.5x; below this, "thin market"
LIQUIDITY_SCORE_GOOD = 15
LIQUIDITY_SCORE_OK = 9
LIQUIDITY_SCORE_MIN = 4
LIQUIDITY_SCORE_BAD = 0

WALLET_AGE_ESTABLISHED = 365
WALLET_AGE_NEW = 90
WALLET_SCORE_AGE_ESTABLISHED = 8
WALLET_SCORE_AGE_NEW = 4
WALLET_SCORE_FLAGS_CLEAN = 7
WALLET_SCORE_FLAGS_ONE = 3

APPROVAL_SCORE_THRESHOLD = 70
MAX_DEBT_CAP = 1_000_000_000  # $1e9, input-validation ceiling per platform


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 1: read inputs. Per-platform existing debt (private, one per party,
# 0 = no position -- also hides membership), then the requester's own
# business-private + pre-computed-public-metric fields.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def read_platform_debts():
    return [sint.get_input_from(i) for i in range(N_PLATFORMS)]


def read_requester_context():
    new_loan = sint.get_input_from(REQUESTER)
    collateral_value = sint.get_input_from(REQUESTER)
    vol_bps = sint.get_input_from(REQUESTER)
    corr_bps = sint.get_input_from(REQUESTER)
    liquidity_ratio_bps = sint.get_input_from(REQUESTER)
    wallet_age_days = sint.get_input_from(REQUESTER)
    wallet_flags = sint.get_input_from(REQUESTER)
    return dict(new_loan=new_loan, collateral_value=collateral_value,
                vol_bps=vol_bps, corr_bps=corr_bps,
                liquidity_ratio_bps=liquidity_ratio_bps,
                wallet_age_days=wallet_age_days, wallet_flags=wallet_flags)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 2: input validation. A platform inputting a negative or absurd debt
# would skew the aggregate -- the protocol's malicious security (where
# available) authenticates the SHARING, not the semantics of what was
# shared. Cheap in-circuit range check, closes that gap.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def validate_debts(platform_debts):
    any_invalid = sint(0)
    for debt in platform_debts:
        valid = (debt >= sint(0)) * (debt <= sint(MAX_DEBT_CAP))
        any_invalid = any_invalid + (sint(1) - valid)
    return (any_invalid > sint(0)).reveal()


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 3: volatility tier -> max LTV + volatility sub-score. Nested
# .if_else() on SECRET comparisons, never revealed individually here --
# only the final aggregated score gets revealed once, at the very end,
# exactly like credit_score.mpc's sub-score functions. That pattern is
# proven safe across all three compile domains; only revealing a COMPOSED
# BOOLEAN decision flag before combining is not (see decide() below).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def volatility_tier(vol_bps):
    is_stable = (vol_bps < sint(VOL_STABLE_BPS))
    is_bluechip = (vol_bps < sint(VOL_BLUECHIP_BPS))
    is_medium = (vol_bps < sint(VOL_MEDIUM_BPS))
    max_ltv_bps = is_stable.if_else(sint(MAX_LTV_STABLE_BPS),
                  is_bluechip.if_else(sint(MAX_LTV_BLUECHIP_BPS),
                  is_medium.if_else(sint(MAX_LTV_MEDIUM_BPS),
                                     sint(MAX_LTV_HIGH_BPS))))
    vol_score = is_stable.if_else(sint(VOL_SCORE_STABLE),
                is_bluechip.if_else(sint(VOL_SCORE_BLUECHIP),
                is_medium.if_else(sint(VOL_SCORE_MEDIUM),
                                   sint(VOL_SCORE_HIGH))))
    return max_ltv_bps, vol_score


def correlation_score(corr_bps):
    low = (corr_bps < sint(CORR_LOW_BPS))
    med = (corr_bps < sint(CORR_MED_BPS))
    return low.if_else(sint(CORR_SCORE_LOW),
           med.if_else(sint(CORR_SCORE_MED), sint(CORR_SCORE_HIGH)))


def liquidity_score(liquidity_ratio_bps):
    good = (liquidity_ratio_bps >= sint(LIQUIDITY_GOOD_BPS))
    ok = (liquidity_ratio_bps >= sint(LIQUIDITY_OK_BPS))
    thin = (liquidity_ratio_bps >= sint(LIQUIDITY_MIN_BPS))
    return good.if_else(sint(LIQUIDITY_SCORE_GOOD),
           ok.if_else(sint(LIQUIDITY_SCORE_OK),
           thin.if_else(sint(LIQUIDITY_SCORE_MIN), sint(LIQUIDITY_SCORE_BAD))))


def wallet_score(wallet_age_days, wallet_flags):
    established = (wallet_age_days > sint(WALLET_AGE_ESTABLISHED))
    new_ish = (wallet_age_days > sint(WALLET_AGE_NEW))
    age_pts = established.if_else(sint(WALLET_SCORE_AGE_ESTABLISHED),
              new_ish.if_else(sint(WALLET_SCORE_AGE_NEW), sint(0)))
    clean = (wallet_flags == sint(0))
    one_flag = (wallet_flags == sint(1))
    flag_pts = clean.if_else(sint(WALLET_SCORE_FLAGS_CLEAN),
               one_flag.if_else(sint(WALLET_SCORE_FLAGS_ONE), sint(0)))
    return age_pts + flag_pts


def leverage_tier_and_score(exposure, collateral_value, max_ltv_bps):
    """exposure/collateral_value vs fractions of the policy budget
    (collateral_value * max_ltv_bps), via cross-multiplication -- no
    secret division anywhere. Returns (tier 0..3, score out of 35);
    tier 0 = most comfortable, 3 = over policy."""
    budget = collateral_value * max_ltv_bps
    half = (exposure * BPS * 2 <= budget)
    three_q = (exposure * BPS * 4 <= budget * 3)
    within = (exposure * BPS <= budget)
    tier = half.if_else(sint(0), three_q.if_else(sint(1),
            within.if_else(sint(2), sint(3))))
    score = half.if_else(sint(35), three_q.if_else(sint(25),
            within.if_else(sint(15), sint(0))))
    return tier, score


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 4: decision. Each condition is revealed INDIVIDUALLY right after its
# comparison -- exactly the tutorial.mpc / credit_score.mpc pattern proven
# portable -- then combined with + / * on the now-clear (regint/cint) flags
# (OR = sum > 0, AND = product), never composed as secret booleans before
# revealing (that silently gave the wrong answer in the binary domain in an
# earlier program in this repo). | and & were tried first since they look
# more natural for boolean combination, but they fail at RUNTIME in the
# binary domain ("Code not defined for instruction CONVINT") even though
# they compile cleanly -- confirmed by isolated minimal probes; + and *
# work correctly in all three domains. Priority: RIFIUTATO > APPROVATO >
# REVIEW MANUALE.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def compute_flags(exposure, collateral_value, max_ltv_bps, liq_threshold_bps,
                   vol_bps, liquidity_ratio_bps, wallet_flags, total_score):
    """The seven SECRET comparisons the decision is built from. Kept secret
    here on purpose -- what each caller does with them differs (see below)."""
    return dict(
        over_liq=(exposure * BPS > collateral_value * liq_threshold_bps),
        over_max=(exposure * BPS > collateral_value * max_ltv_bps),
        flags_bad=(wallet_flags >= sint(2)),
        illiquid=(liquidity_ratio_bps < sint(LIQUIDITY_MIN_BPS)),
        vol_extreme=(vol_bps > sint(15000)),
        score_high=(total_score >= sint(APPROVAL_SCORE_THRESHOLD)),
        ample=(exposure * BPS * 10 <= collateral_value * max_ltv_bps * 8),
    )


def decide_public(flags, r_any_invalid):
    """Default, portable-everywhere path: reveal each flag INDIVIDUALLY
    right after its comparison, then combine the already-revealed
    (regint/cint) flags with + (OR, as sum > 0) / * (AND, as product).
    Composing secret booleans arithmetically before revealing is the one
    thing NOT to do here -- it silently gave the wrong answer in the binary
    domain in an earlier program in this repo. Returns already-revealed
    reject/approve for public branching."""
    r_over_liq = flags['over_liq'].reveal()
    r_over_max = flags['over_max'].reveal()
    r_flags_bad = flags['flags_bad'].reveal()
    r_illiquid = flags['illiquid'].reveal()
    r_vol_extreme = flags['vol_extreme'].reveal()
    r_score_high = flags['score_high'].reveal()
    r_ample = flags['ample'].reveal()

    reject_sum = (r_over_liq + r_over_max + r_flags_bad + r_illiquid
                  + r_vol_extreme + r_any_invalid)
    reject = (reject_sum > 0)
    approve = (r_score_high * r_ample)
    return reject, approve


def decide_strict(flags, r_any_invalid):
    """MODE_STRICT (field/ring only, see main()): compose in SECRET
    arithmetic and reveal only the final code, only to the requester.
    Arithmetic composition-then-reveal is fine here specifically because
    this path never runs in the binary domain -- the bug that ruled it
    out for decide_public() above was binary-domain-specific."""
    not_over_liq = sint(1) - flags['over_liq']
    not_over_max = sint(1) - flags['over_max']
    not_flags_bad = sint(1) - flags['flags_bad']
    not_illiquid = sint(1) - flags['illiquid']
    not_vol_extreme = sint(1) - flags['vol_extreme']
    not_invalid = sint(1) - sint(r_any_invalid)
    all_clear = (not_over_liq * not_over_max * not_flags_bad
                 * not_illiquid * not_vol_extreme * not_invalid)
    reject_s = sint(1) - all_clear
    approve_s = flags['score_high'] * flags['ample']
    return reject_s, approve_s


def decide_production(flags, r_any_invalid):
    """MODE_PRODUCTION (field/ring only, see main()): identical secret-
    arithmetic composition to decide_strict -- the 7 flags must never
    exist in the clear to any party, not even transiently, which rules
    out decide_public()'s reveal-then-combine pattern here too. The only
    difference from decide_strict is what main() does with the result
    afterwards: broadcast via plain .reveal() to all N parties instead of
    reveal_to(REQUESTER) alone, so every participating platform can
    independently verify and sign the same outcome. Kept as a separate
    function (rather than reusing decide_strict) so that distinction is
    explicit at the call site in main(), not implicit in how the caller
    happens to use a shared helper's return value."""
    not_over_liq = sint(1) - flags['over_liq']
    not_over_max = sint(1) - flags['over_max']
    not_flags_bad = sint(1) - flags['flags_bad']
    not_illiquid = sint(1) - flags['illiquid']
    not_vol_extreme = sint(1) - flags['vol_extreme']
    not_invalid = sint(1) - sint(r_any_invalid)
    all_clear = (not_over_liq * not_over_max * not_flags_bad
                 * not_illiquid * not_vol_extreme * not_invalid)
    reject_s = sint(1) - all_clear
    approve_s = flags['score_high'] * flags['ample']
    return reject_s, approve_s


def main():
    platform_debts = read_platform_debts()
    ctx = read_requester_context()

    r_any_invalid = validate_debts(platform_debts)

    existing_debt = sum(platform_debts, sint(0))
    exposure = existing_debt + ctx['new_loan']  # post-issuance leverage

    max_ltv_bps, vol_score = volatility_tier(ctx['vol_bps'])
    liq_threshold_bps = max_ltv_bps + sint(LIQ_BUFFER_BPS)
    # thin markets liquidate worse: tighten the buffer
    thin = (ctx['liquidity_ratio_bps'] < sint(LIQUIDITY_MIN_BPS))
    liq_threshold_bps = thin.if_else(liq_threshold_bps - sint(500), liq_threshold_bps)

    lev_tier, lev_score = leverage_tier_and_score(
        exposure, ctx['collateral_value'], max_ltv_bps)
    corr_score = correlation_score(ctx['corr_bps'])
    liq_score = liquidity_score(ctx['liquidity_ratio_bps'])
    wal_score = wallet_score(ctx['wallet_age_days'], ctx['wallet_flags'])

    total_score = lev_score + vol_score + corr_score + liq_score + wal_score

    flags = compute_flags(exposure, ctx['collateral_value'], max_ltv_bps,
                           liq_threshold_bps, ctx['vol_bps'],
                           ctx['liquidity_ratio_bps'], ctx['wallet_flags'],
                           total_score)

    # MODE is compile-time-known (a plain int from program.args, resolved
    # before any of this runs), and program.options.binary is too, so
    # these are plain Python branches -- exactly one body ends up in the
    # compiled bytecode, never a runtime branch on a compile-time value.
    # The compile-time guard above already rejects MODE != 0 for binary,
    # so "not program.options.binary" here is defensive, not load-bearing.
    if MODE == MODE_STRICT and not program.options.binary:
        # Nothing but the final code is revealed, and only to the
        # requester: no sub-scores, no aggregate, no public branching
        # (branching needs an openly-known condition by construction, so
        # a real APPROVATO/RIFIUTATO print from inside MPC is incompatible
        # with hiding the outcome from the other platforms -- the caller
        # has to map the numeric code to a label itself, after the reveal).
        reject_s, approve_s = decide_strict(flags, r_any_invalid)
        decision_code = reject_s.if_else(sint(0), approve_s.if_else(sint(2), sint(1)))
        print_ln_to(REQUESTER, 'policy_version: %s', POLICY_VERSION)
        print_ln_to(REQUESTER, 'esito_code_strict: %s',
                    decision_code.reveal_to(REQUESTER))
        return

    if MODE == MODE_PRODUCTION and not program.options.binary:
        # All N parties learn the same numbers -- enough to verify and
        # co-sign one outcome -- and nothing else: no sub-scores, no raw
        # flags. max_ltv_bps AND liq_threshold_bps are both safe to
        # reveal here: both derive only from the requester's own public
        # volatility figure plus PUBLIC policy constants (LIQ_BUFFER_BPS,
        # the thin-market adjustment) -- neither touches the
        # cross-platform debt data that is the actual thing this
        # program protects. An earlier revision withheld
        # liq_threshold_bps here on the mistaken assumption that
        # withholding more was automatically safer, without checking
        # whether it actually derived from the protected secret (it does
        # not) -- the only real cost of that choice was a needlessly
        # noisy liquidation-price estimate downstream (see
        # Scripts/risk_data/policy.py), not any privacy gain. A signer
        # needs both figures downstream to derive an accurate indicative
        # liquidation price (see policy.py's N==2 conservative estimate,
        # needed because the exact exposure below is withheld at N==2).
        reject_s, approve_s = decide_production(flags, r_any_invalid)
        decision_code = reject_s.if_else(sint(0), approve_s.if_else(sint(2), sint(1)))
        r_decision = decision_code.reveal()
        r_score = total_score.reveal()
        r_max_ltv = max_ltv_bps.reveal()
        r_liq_threshold = liq_threshold_bps.reveal()
        print_ln('policy_version: %s', POLICY_VERSION)
        print_ln('esito_code: %s', r_decision)
        print_ln('risk_score: %s', r_score)
        print_ln('max_ltv_bps: %s', r_max_ltv)
        print_ln('liq_threshold_bps: %s', r_liq_threshold)
        # Same N==2-vs-N>=3 rule as MODE_BENCHMARK, no exception: giving
        # even REQUESTER the exact aggregate at N==2 would let it recover
        # the other platform's exact debt by subtraction (REQUESTER
        # already knows its own contribution and the new loan) -- the
        # risk here does not depend on who the reveal target is, only on
        # whether the exact number ever becomes public at all.
        if N_PLATFORMS == 2:
            print_ln('esposizione_totale: fascia %s (0=comoda..3=oltre policy)',
                      lev_tier.reveal())
        else:
            print_ln('esposizione_totale_usd: %s', exposure.reveal())
        return

    reject, approve = decide_public(flags, r_any_invalid)

    r_max_ltv = max_ltv_bps.reveal()
    r_liq_threshold = liq_threshold_bps.reveal()
    r_score = total_score.reveal()

    print_ln('--- valori calcolati ---')
    print_ln('policy_version: %s', POLICY_VERSION)
    print_ln('piattaforme: %s', N_PLATFORMS)

    # N=2: revealing the exact aggregate would let the requester recover
    # the other platform's exact debt by subtracting its own -- reveal
    # only the leverage tier. N>=3: no single platform can isolate
    # another's contribution from the sum, safe to reveal exactly.
    if N_PLATFORMS == 2:
        r_lev = lev_tier.reveal()
        print_ln('esposizione_totale: fascia %s (0=comoda..3=oltre policy)', r_lev)
    else:
        r_exposure = exposure.reveal()
        print_ln('esposizione_totale_usd: %s', r_exposure)

    print_ln('max_ltv_bps: %s', r_max_ltv)
    print_ln('liq_threshold_bps: %s', r_liq_threshold)
    print_ln('punteggio_leva: %s', lev_score.reveal())
    print_ln('punteggio_volatilita: %s', vol_score.reveal())
    print_ln('punteggio_correlazione: %s', corr_score.reveal())
    print_ln('punteggio_liquidita: %s', liq_score.reveal())
    print_ln('punteggio_wallet: %s', wal_score.reveal())
    print_ln('risk_score: %s', r_score)

    @if_e(reject)
    def _():
        print_ln('esito: RIFIUTATO')
        print_ln('motivazione: sopra la soglia di liquidazione o il LTV massimo, '
                  'oppure segnali on-chain negativi, mercato troppo illiquido, '
                  'volatilita estrema, o input di una piattaforma non valido')
    @else_
    def _():
        @if_e(approve)
        def _():
            print_ln('esito: APPROVATO')
            print_ln('motivazione: punteggio sopra soglia e leva ampiamente '
                      'entro policy')
        @else_
        def _():
            print_ln('esito: REVIEW MANUALE')
            print_ln('motivazione: nessun motivo di rifiuto ma punteggio o '
                      'margine di leva insufficienti per approvazione automatica')


main()
